<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
        <artifactId>rains-flexible-modules</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>moderation-standalone</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Rain's Moderation</name>
    <description>
        Rain's Moderation, as a plugin of its own. Nothing here but a build file and a descriptor: the
        moderation module and the standard wrapper are shaded together, and that is the whole plugin.
        The name and the data folder are unchanged from the standalone plugin it replaces.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!--
          The name the server sees, and the name of its data folder. Kept as it was: an upgrading
          server has plugins/RainsModeration/ and a renamed plugin would come up looking at an empty
          folder.
        -->
        <plugin.name>RainsModeration</plugin.name>

        <!--
          Rain's Moderation's own version line, continued — not the reactor's.

          2.0.0 rather than 1.1.0 because this is the plugin rebuilt on a new foundation: the
          punishments, the vanish, the inventory viewing and the audit trail all moved into RainsCore,
          and what is left here is the commands, the screens and the product decisions. Nothing an
          owner configured is lost, but a major number is what says "read the notes before upgrading".

          2.12.0: a permission an admin individually took away from a staff member — through the per-
          person toggle, rank left alone — came straight back the next time that person logged in,
          because the join top-up that hands out nodes a preset has gained since somebody's promotion
          used to do it by fully re-applying the preset, wiping every individual toggle in the process
          rather than only adding what was actually new. It now remembers which nodes were explicitly
          refused and leaves them refused; only "put the preset back" on the rank screen, the
          deliberate full reset, still undoes those on purpose.

          2.13.0: /mod <player> now shows playtime, first-joined, last-seen and a handful of combat
          statistics, read straight from Minecraft's own per-player records — nothing new is tracked
          for this, so it answers for somebody offline exactly as well as somebody standing in front
          of you. Alongside it, a persisted per-player "x-ray probability" — a long-running, restart-
          surviving average of both the ore ratio and the mining-directness signal from 2.12.0's
          review screen, shown on the player's own page and ranked across everybody who has ever
          mined anything on a new /mod xray leaderboard. Never a verdict on its own: clicking anybody
          on the leaderboard opens the same block-by-block review a report's own investigation would,
          so the number is never the last thing anybody sees before deciding what to do with it.

          2.14.0: the review screen used to lose every found ore on a restart even though the
          probability survived one — a moderator could see "72%" and open the review to find nothing
          behind it. The actual findings are persisted now too, capped per player rather than kept
          for good, closing that gap. The leaderboard also only lists players who have actually been
          found mining a watched ore now, rather than anybody who has ever broken a single block of
          anything. And "Which blocks count as valuable" has a proper picker — /mod xray → Watched
          ores — a grid of nineteen common ores to click through instead of typing material names
          into chat one comma at a time; both write the exact same setting.

          2.15.0: ore already exposed on two or more faces before anybody touched it — a lush cave, a
          ravine wall, an ordinary cavern — no longer counts as a find at all. The whole premise behind
          both the ratio and the mining-directness signal is ore reached by digging through rock that
          hid it; ore somebody simply walked up to was never hidden, and reporting it was a false
          positive on a player exploring the way the game is meant to be explored. Alongside it, a new
          "VeinMiner mode" setting (/mod xray, or xray.veinminer-mode in config) treats every same-
          material ore block broken within a quarter second of the last one credited to the same player
          as the tail of one vein-mining click rather than a separate find, so a server running a
          VeinMiner-style plugin no longer turns one deposit into a wall of separate suspicious events.

          2.16.0: 2.15.0's exposure check asked for two open faces before exempting anything, on the
          assumption that a cave-wall ore block sits in fairly open space — but a vein embedded in a
          cave wall usually only borders the cavity on the one face actually facing it, same as a
          straight tunnel dug at hidden ore only ever opens the one face it was just broken through
          from. Counting faces alone cannot tell those two apart. It now instead remembers a player's
          own last several breaks and asks whether an open face was already open before they touched
          anything, rather than open because they just mined it themselves: a single pre-existing
          opening is enough to exempt a find, and a face the player dug through their own tunnel a
          moment ago never is, however many or few of the six sides end up open either way.
        -->
        <plugin.version>2.16.0</plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>moderation-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>modules-wrapper</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <finalName>${plugin.name}-${plugin.version}</finalName>
        <resources>
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
                <!-- The descriptor names the version, and a version written twice is a version that drifts. -->
                <filtering>true</filtering>
            </resource>
        </resources>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
              StandaloneJarTest reads the jar this build produces, so it has to run after it exists.
              Surefire bound to a later phase rather than failsafe, which is one more plugin to have
              cached — this build has to work offline.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>default-test</id>
                        <phase>none</phase>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>after-the-jar-exists</id>
                        <phase>integration-test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>test</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <skipTests>false</skipTests>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
